import{_ as a,o as t,c as o,a0 as r}from"./chunks/framework.K3sFe4KB.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Plan, apply and drift","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"explanation/plan-apply-drift.md","filePath":"explanation/plan-apply-drift.md"}'),d={name:"explanation/plan-apply-drift.md"};function i(n,e,s,c,l,p){return t(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="plan-apply-and-drift" tabindex="-1">Plan, apply and drift <a class="header-anchor" href="#plan-apply-and-drift" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Plan, apply and drift&quot;">​</a></h1><p>cicada borrows the infrastructure-as-code lifecycle and adapts it to calendars, whose APIs are not transactional. This page is the mental model: what the three states are, why apply is safe by construction, and what the contracts guarantee.</p><h2 id="three-states" tabindex="-1">Three states <a class="header-anchor" href="#three-states" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Three states&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Desired</strong> — what your YAML declares.</li><li><strong>Recorded</strong> — <code>.cicada/state.yaml</code>: the last reconciled state, keyed by your stable local ids, plus the apply journal.</li><li><strong>Live</strong> — what the provider actually has right now.</li></ul><p>The <strong>stable local id</strong> (<code>id: standup</code>) is the anchor. Export-only calendar tools fail precisely here: without stable ids mapped to remote ids, a re-run can only create duplicates. Recorded state is what lets cicada update and delete, not just create.</p><ul><li><strong>Plan</strong> diffs desired vs recorded (what you changed) <em>and</em> recorded vs live (what changed out-of-band) into ordered operations: <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>replace</code>, <code>noop</code>.</li><li><strong>Apply</strong> executes the plan and persists new recorded state.</li><li><strong>Drift</strong> is recorded ≠ live — the remote edit a colleague made in the provider UI.</li></ul><h2 id="the-managed-boundary" tabindex="-1">The managed boundary <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-managed-boundary" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The managed boundary&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The managed set is <em>exactly</em> recorded state. Consequences:</p><ul><li><code>delete</code> ops are generated only for local ids recorded but absent from your YAML.</li><li>Live events unknown to recorded state are never modified or deleted — a human&#39;s lunch on a managed calendar is untouchable.</li><li>Two cicada sources (two repos, an agent and a human) can share one provider calendar, each reconciling only its own recorded slice.</li></ul><p>Every event cicada writes carries provider-side tags — on Google, <code>extendedProperties.private[&quot;cicada:id&quot;]</code> plus <code>cicada:managed</code> and a <code>cicada:state</code> content hash. Tags, not guesses, delimit the boundary: they enable state rebuild, adoption reporting, and safe resume.</p><h2 id="apply-is-sequenced-idempotent-resumable" tabindex="-1">Apply is sequenced, idempotent, resumable <a class="header-anchor" href="#apply-is-sequenced-idempotent-resumable" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Apply is sequenced, idempotent, resumable&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Calendar APIs have no transactions, so cicada is honest about partiality instead of faking atomicity:</p><ul><li>Ops run in a fixed order — <code>delete</code>, <code>replace</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>noop</code> — minimising conflicts.</li><li>Each op carries an idempotency key (<code>calendarId | localId | opKind | contentHash</code>); providers that honour request ids use it.</li><li>State is written <strong>per op</strong>, as a journal inside the versioned state document. A crash mid-apply leaves a valid, resumable file.</li><li><code>apply --resume</code> skips journaled ops. And because the failure window is &quot;provider write landed, journal write crashed&quot;, resume also reconciles by <code>cicada:id</code> tag — a <code>create</code>/<code>replace</code> that finds its tag already live is journalled <code>skippedAlreadyApplied</code>, not duplicated. This check runs on every apply, which is also how a lost state file gets rebuilt.</li><li>A failed op is journalled <code>failed</code> with its error kind and does not abort the run — the report carries every outcome.</li></ul><p>Apply is <strong>convergent, not atomic</strong>. There is no rollback; there is resume.</p><h2 id="conflict-and-notification-policy" tabindex="-1">Conflict and notification policy <a class="header-anchor" href="#conflict-and-notification-policy" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Conflict and notification policy&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Apply performs an implicit refresh of the managed set first. If live diverged from recorded for an event the plan touches:</p><ul><li><strong>Default:</strong> the op fails with a <code>conflict</code> error; state is untouched for it. <code>cicada refresh</code> + re-plan resolves.</li><li><strong><code>--force</code>:</strong> desired wins; the live edit is overwritten (logged). The recorded etag is dropped so the write lands unconditionally.</li></ul><p>Where the provider supports conditional writes (Google: etag → <code>If-Match</code>), <code>replace</code>/<code>update</code> carry the recorded etag — optimistic concurrency where available, conflict checking otherwise.</p><p>Providers email attendees on writes, so <code>sendUpdates</code> is a first-class knob (<code>spec.apply.sendUpdates</code>, <code>--notify</code>, the <code>plan.apply</code> parameter) defaulting to <code>externalOnly</code> — drift repairs shouldn&#39;t spam the team.</p><h2 id="versioned-public-contracts" tabindex="-1">Versioned public contracts <a class="header-anchor" href="#versioned-public-contracts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Versioned public contracts&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The state file (<code>cicada/state-v1</code>) and plan output (<code>cicada/plan-v1</code> via <code>plan --out</code>) are versioned documents that CI and agents can depend on; migrations are explicit. Plan findings carry severity — <code>warning</code> informs, <code>error</code> blocks apply (capability gaps surface before anything writes).</p><h2 id="why-this-shape" tabindex="-1">Why this shape <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-this-shape" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why this shape&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The alternatives were rejected for concrete reasons: Terraform couples the core to a plugin RPC model; export-only tools can&#39;t update, delete, or detect drift; heuristic stateless matching (summary + time) is fragile for exactly the updates and deletes that matter. Stable ids plus recorded state is the only sound foundation for idempotent apply — and the journal is what makes an untransactional world resumable anyway.</p>',23)])])}const f=a(d,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,f as default};
